hypotheses.statement:
Let $E$ be a subset of a separable metric space and let $s>0$. If $$\label{frames:vanishing-content}
  \mathcal H^s_\infty(E\cap B(x,r))=o(r^s)
  \quad\text{as }r\downarrow0\quad\text{for every }x\in E,$$ then $\mathcal H^s(E)=0$. It suffices to assume <a href="#frames:vanishing-content" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="frames:vanishing-content">[frames:vanishing-content]</a> along all sufficiently small dyadic radii.

Let $(M,d,m)$ be a full-support $\mathrm{RCD}(K,N)$ space, where $K\in\mathbb R$ and $1<N<\infty$. Let $F$ be bounded and globally Lipschitz, and let $G$ be bounded and continuous. Suppose that for every compactly supported $g\in\operatorname{Test}(M)$ and every nonnegative $h\in\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}_c(M)$, $$\label{weight:hessian-assumption}
 H_F(\nabla g,\nabla g)(h)
 \le \int hG|\nabla g|^2\,\mathrm dm,$$ where the weak Hessian is defined by <a href="#eq:weak-hessian" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:weak-hessian">[eq:weak-hessian]</a>. Then every constant-speed minimizing geodesic $\sigma:[0,1]\to M$, of length $\ell$, satisfies $$\label{weight:curve-hessian}
 (F\circ\sigma)''\le\ell^2G\circ\sigma
 \quad\text{in distributions on }(0,1).$$

