Let $(M,d)$ be a complete $n$-dimensional Alexandrov space with curvature bounded below by $\kappa$, where $n\geq2$, and put $m=\mathcal H^n_d$. Then $(M,d,m)$ is $\mathrm{RCD}((n-1)\kappa,n)$, its measure has full support, and $$\label{fwd:target}
 R(X,Y,Y,X)(f)
 \geq \kappa\int f\bigl(|X|^2|Y|^2-\langle X,Y\rangle^2\bigr)\,\mathrm dm$$ for every nonnegative $f\in\operatorname{Test}(M)$ and every $X,Y\in\operatorname{TestV}(M)$, with the global test classes and curvature convention of Theorem <a href="#main:equivalence" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="main:equivalence">1</a>.

The flows above exist and are invertible modulo null sets. Their forward and inverse compression constants are $1+O(h)$, their displacements are $O(h)$, and their transported densities have support in a common compact set. Uniformly in $r\in[0,1]$, $$\label{fwd:flow-density-bound}
 \|\rho^h_{r,A}-f\|_{L^\infty}=O(h).$$ Moreover, $$\label{fwd:flow-first}
 \frac{\rho^h_{r,A}-f}{h}\rightharpoonup^* rL_A$$ in $L^\infty$ on the common bounded support, both at each fixed $r$ and on $[0,1]\times M$. For every fixed $q\in W^{1,2}(M)$, $$\label{fwd:flow-second}
 \int q\rho^h_A
 =\int qf+h\int qL_A
 +\frac{h^2}{2}\int
   \langle\nabla q,fS_A+L_AA\rangle+o(h^2).$$ If $g$ is Lipschitz on a neighborhood of the common support, then $$\label{fwd:increment}
 \frac{g(F^h_{1,A}(z))-g(z)}{h}
 \longrightarrow dg(A)(z)
 \quad\text{in }L^1(fm).$$

For the above centered finite law and flows, put $$a^h_A(z):=d(z,F^h_{1,A}(z)),\qquad
 e^h_{AB}(z):=d(F^h_{1,A}(z),F^h_{1,B}(z)).$$ Then $$\label{fwd:distance-first}
 \frac{a^h_A}{h}\longrightarrow |A|,\qquad
 \frac{e^h_{AB}}h\longrightarrow |A-B|$$ in measure and in every finite $L^p(fm)$, for each choice of $A,B$. Furthermore, $$\label{fwd:energy-comparison}
 \liminf_{h\downarrow0}
 \frac{2\mathbb E J^h_{0A}-\mathbb E J^h_{AB}}{h^4}
 \geq\frac\kappa6\int f\,\mathbb E|A\wedge B|^2.$$

Put $D=B-A$ and $C_s=(1-s)A+sB$. The densities $\sigma^h_s$ have common compact support and a uniform $L^\infty$ bound. They satisfy $$\label{fwd:density-bound}
 \sup_{0\leq s\leq1}\|\sigma^h_s-f\|_{L^2}^2=O(h^2),
 \qquad
 \frac{\sigma^h_s-f}{h}\rightharpoonup L_{C_s}
 \quad\text{in }L^2(\,\mathrm ds\,\mathrm dm).$$ Their velocities satisfy $$\label{fwd:velocity-first}
 \int_0^1\int\sigma^h_s|v^h_s/h-D|^2\,\mathrm ds\longrightarrow0.$$

Let $f\geq0$ be compactly supported and Lipschitz, and let $V_1,\ldots,V_N\in L^2(TM)$. For every $\eta>0$, there are finitely many nonnegative Lipschitz functions $\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_L$, with $\sum_{\ell=1}^L\alpha_\ell=1$, and functions $q_{r,\ell}\in W^{1,2}(M)$, such that $$\label{fwd:selection-error}
 \sum_{r=1}^N\sum_{\ell=1}^L
 \int f\alpha_\ell|\nabla q_{r,\ell}-V_r|^2<\eta.$$ The products $f\alpha_\ell$ are compactly supported Lipschitz functions. No bound for $\nabla\alpha_\ell$ independent of $\eta$ is asserted or needed.

Let $P_s=\exp(s\Delta)$. On $0<s\le S<\infty$ one has $$\begin{aligned}
\label{heat:bakry-one}
|\nabla P_sh|&\le e^{-Ks}P_s|\nabla h|,\\
\label{heat:reverse-poincare}
|\nabla P_sh|^2&\le C_Ss^{-1}P_s(h^2),\\
\label{heat:laplacian-estimate}
|\Delta P_sh|^2&\le C_Ss^{-1}P_s(|\nabla h|^2),
\end{aligned}$$ with the usual Sobolev hypothesis for the first and third inequalities and $h\in L^2$ for the second. In particular, $$\label{heat:laplacian-from-values}
|\Delta P_sh|^2\le C_Ss^{-2}P_s(h^2).$$ Positive-time heat regularization of an $L^2\cap L^\infty$ function has bounded Lipschitz representatives for all its generator powers. These assertions extend to bounded data, by local approximation, whenever the right-hand sides are defined by the heat kernel.*


*For every bounded nonnegative, nonzero datum $h$, and $0<s\le1$, the locally defined logarithmic Laplacian satisfies $$\label{heat:li-yau-consequence}
\Delta\log P_sh\ge
-C\left(s^{-1}+s|\nabla\log P_sh|^2\right).$$ All logarithmic statements are local on regions where the positive-time solution has a positive lower bound.

For each fixed $c>0$, on compact spatial sets and compact time intervals in $(0,1)$, the factors in <a href="#heat:two-factors" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="heat:two-factors">[heat:two-factors]</a> have bounded Lipschitz generator powers and positive lower bounds. Their logarithms are local test functions, with locally bounded Sobolev Laplacians. The density is a positive local test function. Time differentiation of the potentials is valid locally in the Laplacian graph norm, hence also for their $L^2$ Hessians.*





*There is a constant $L$, independent of $c$ and $t$, such that $$\label{heat:uniform-gradients}
|\nabla\phi|+|\nabla\psi|+|v_c|+|w_c|\le L.$$ The following identities hold on $(0,1)$: $$\begin{aligned}
\label{heat:continuity}
\partial_t\rho&=-\mathop{\mathrm{div}}(\rho v_c),&
\nabla\rho&=\rho w_c/c,\\
\label{heat:hamilton-jacobi}
\partial_t\theta&=-\tfrac12(|v_c|^2+|w_c|^2)-c\Delta b,&
\partial_t\phi&=-\tfrac12|\nabla\phi|^2+c\Delta\phi.
\end{aligned}$$ Moreover, $\int\rho_t\,\mathrm dm=1$, $\phi_t$ is uniformly bounded, and, for fixed $c$, as $t\uparrow1$, $$\label{heat:terminal-traces}
\rho_t\longrightarrow f,\qquad
\theta_t\longrightarrow\phi_1+c\log f
\quad\text{locally uniformly and strongly in }W^{1,2}_{\rm loc}.$$ The local Lipschitz bounds for $\rho_t$ near the endpoint may depend on the fixed $c$. The bound for $\nabla\theta_t$ is uniform for $0<c\le1$, and the terminal density is always the fixed function $f$.

For each $A<\infty$ there is $C_A<\infty$ such that, if $0<s\le1$, $g>0$ is bounded and $\mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}(\log g)\sqrt{s}\le A$, then $$\label{heat:kernel-ratio-estimate}
\frac{(P_sg^2)^{1/2}}{P_sg}\le C_A
\qquad\text{everywhere.}$$ The constant is independent of the base point and of a positive lower bound for $g$.

As $c\downarrow0$, $$\label{heat:potential-limit}
\phi_t^c\longrightarrow\Phi_t=Q_tu
\quad\text{locally uniformly on }(0,1]\times M.$$ At each fixed positive time the convergence is also weak in $W^{1,2}_{\rm loc}$.

Along a subsequence as in Lemma <a href="#heat:curve-compactness" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="heat:curve-compactness">28</a>, there is a measurable vector field $V_t$, with $|V_t|\le L$ $\nu_t\,\mathrm dt\,\mathrm dm$-almost everywhere, such that $\partial_t\nu+\mathop{\mathrm{div}}(\nu V)=0$. For every $0<\alpha<\beta\le1$, $$\begin{aligned}
\label{heat:exact-action-identity}
\left[\int\phi_t^c\rho_t^c\,\mathrm dm\right]_\alpha^\beta
&=\frac12\int_\alpha^\beta\!\int
\rho_t^c|\nabla\phi_t^c|^2\,\mathrm dm\,\mathrm dt\notag\\
&=\frac12\int_\alpha^\beta\!\int
\rho_t^c|v_c|^2\,\mathrm dm\,\mathrm dt+o(1).
\end{aligned}$$ The limiting curves are constant-speed Wasserstein geodesics on these intervals, and $$\label{heat:strong-weighted-velocity}
\int_\alpha^\beta\!\int
\rho_t^c|v_c-V_t|^2\,\mathrm dm\,\mathrm dt\longrightarrow0.$$ For every fixed $L^2$ tensor coefficient $T$, compactly supported in space-time in $[\alpha,\beta]\times M$, $$\label{heat:strong-coefficient-velocity}
\int_\alpha^\beta\!\int
\rho_t^c|T(v_c-V_t)|^2\,\mathrm dm\,\mathrm dt\longrightarrow0.$$

Let $E$ be a subset of a separable metric space and let $s>0$. If $$\label{frames:vanishing-content}
  \mathcal H^s_\infty(E\cap B(x,r))=o(r^s)
  \quad\text{as }r\downarrow0\quad\text{for every }x\in E,$$ then $\mathcal H^s(E)=0$. It suffices to assume <a href="#frames:vanishing-content" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="frames:vanishing-content">[frames:vanishing-content]</a> along all sufficiently small dyadic radii.

Work in a bounded region where <a href="#eq:local-volume" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:local-volume">[eq:local-volume]</a> and the Poincaré inequality hold with fixed constants. There are an enlargement factor $L$ and $\varepsilon_0>0$ with the following property. Let $P$ be a bounded Sobolev matrix on $B(x,Lr)$ and suppose $$\int_{B(x,Lr)}|P-I|\,\mathrm dm\leq\varepsilon_0r^n.$$ For $n-2<s$ let $E\subset B(x,r)$ consist of precise-representative points of $P$ at which $|P-I|\geq1$. Then $$\label{frames:energy-content-bound}
  \mathcal H^s_\infty(E)
   \leq C_s r^{s+2-n}
             \int_{B(x,Lr)}|\nabla P|^2\,\mathrm dm.$$ The constants depend only on $s,n$ and the local volume and Poincaré constants.

