Assume the conclusion of Theorem <a href="#frames:hessian" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="frames:hessian">40</a>, and write $R_*=R_\kappa>0$ for its radius. There are numbers $0<a<b<R_*$, depending only on $\kappa$ and the chosen $R_*$, such that for every $p\in M$ there are bounded globally Lipschitz $F_p$ and bounded continuous $G_p$ satisfying <a href="#weight:hessian-assumption" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="weight:hessian-assumption">[weight:hessian-assumption]</a> and $$\label{weight:local-modification}
F_p=\mathfrak m_\kappa(d(p,\cdot)),\qquad
G_p=1-\kappa F_p\quad\text{on }B_a(p).$$ For $\kappa>0$, we may require $b<\pi/(2\sqrt\kappa)$.
